abstract={The standing crop biomass of "producer" and "consumer" organisms of a windward, interisland coral reef was estimated in mid-summer 1954, and primary productivity and total respiration determined by upstream and down-stream chemical measurements. Because algae are so intimately interwoven with animal and dead skeletal material chlorophyll extraction was used to estimate "producer" biomass, algal dry weight being determined by relating spectrophotometrically chlorophyll content with known dry weight of a reference species of Codium. On a horizontal surface area basis the average living coral colony proved to contain three times as much plant as animal tissue (0.063 gms/ cm2 dry wt. algae as compared with 0.021 of animal polyp). Zooxanthellae comprised only 6% of plant portion, filamentous green algae embedded in the skeleton making up the bulk. Although considered "parasitic" by former authors, the evidence strongly indicates that skeletal algae are "mutalistic", the algal-coelenterate complex being a highly intergrated ecological unit permitting cyclic use and reuse of food and nutrients. A pyramid of biomass and similar standing crop values were found in all six major zones of the reef. The mean standing crop for the whole reef was producers, 703; herbivores, 132; and carnivores, 11 g/m2. A very high production of about 74,000 lbs./acre/year was obtained by the flow rate method indicating that the standing crop "turns over" about 12.5 times per year, despite the low nutrient level of water passing over the reef. Efficiency of primary production was estimated to be 6%. Production was found to balance respiration suggesting that the reef community, under present water level conditions, is a true ecological climax or open steady state system. Measurement of low-level radioactivity which was present provided further evidence of nutrient conservation and symbiosis between corals and their skeletal algae.},
