abstract={This study describes the feeding ecology of an orbiniid polychaete, Leitoscoloplos fragilis Verrill, that is patchily distributed in the lower intertidal sands of Cape Henlopen, Delaware [USA]. The abundances of worms and potential food items (organic POC/PON, diatoms, bacteria) and several sedimentological and geochemical measurements were followed for 1 yr at two stations (designated HI and LO). Population densities of L. fragilis were significantly higher at Station HI throughout the year. The abundance of fine particles (< 125 .mu.m) was always higher at Station LO. Activity-dependent prfolies of the particle radiotracer Be-7 suggested that L. fragilis did not significantly affect sediment turnover. Subsurface peaks in Be-7 activity-depth profiles were deeper in November than in August because of more intense physical disturbance. A greater abundance of fine particles are buried more rapidly at depth in August because the overall sediment transport and removal of fine particles are lower at this time of year. Standing stocks of bacteria and diatoms at each station were not significantly different. Microbenthic metabolic activity and ammonia production (from worm excretion and remineralization) were significantly higher at station HI. In these coarse-grained sediments benthic diatoms are the major food resource for L. fragilis. A greater abundance of fines in this environment may impede the upward flux of the primary inorganic nitrogen source (ammonia) to surface diatoms. Benthic diatom production may be the major factor that controls population density of L. fragilis in sediment at Cape Henlopen.},
