abstract={At the concentration of 0.125 mM, the purine hypoxanthine is shown to be a good and energy-efficient nitrogen source for phototrophic growth of at least 60% of 26 tested species of marine microalgae from 10 taxonomic classes. At 10-fold higher concentration, this purine generally augmented growth of the species showing fair-to-good growth on the low concentration, and this growth increase was dramatic for most flagellates. However, the higher concentration level caused weak or no growth improvement of the species showing poor purine utilization at the lower level. There was no indication of hypoxanthine concentration toxicity, and the maximal growth yields achieved by the best purine utilizers indicated assimilation of all 4 N atoms per molecule of hypoxanthine. These results are combined with the recently reported excretion of hypoxanthine-guanine by marine ciliates to suggest a short-circuited N-turnover cycle in certain planktonic associations whereby the excreted purines may be directly reutilized by a large proportion of marine phytoplankton.},
