start_page={5},
end_page={32},
abstract={Recent research on Philippine bivalves revealed several species hitherto unknown for harboring microorganisms which contribute to the nutrition of their hosts. At present we can discern mutualistic associations of two kinds in four different groups of Eulamellibranchia from the Philippines, all belonging to the Heterodonta-Veneroida: firstly endosymbiotic algae (zooxanthellae) in Tridacnidae (Tridacnacea) and Fraginae (Cardiacea), secondly endocytosymbiotic bacteria in Lucinidae and Fimbriidae (Lucinacea). Though far from being fully understood, the Tridacnidae-algae association is comparatively well-studied, and was the only one known from Philippine waters. Present knowledge on the tridacnids is briefly reviewed and a survey on the quite different Fraginae is given. The bacterial symbioses described here comprise four species (Codakia tigerina {locally: "tambayan, " "libuo, " or "bakalan"}; Anodontia edentula {"imbaw"}; Fimbria fimbriata {"bug-atan"}) and one family (Fimbriidae), as yet not known for their symbiotrophic nutrition. Since there are more lucinid species known from Philippine waters, the presence of further bacterial symbioses is predicted. While symbiotic algae represent a photosynthecially-fueled source of energy for the host, bacteria are chemoautotrophs, gaining energy from the oxidation of sulfides. All bivalve species mentioned traditionally serve as food-stuff in the Philippines. At present only tridacnids are envisaged for mariculture. However, the potential of the other species for mariculture, in particular of those with a chemoautotrophically supported metabolism, is high, too. There is the perspective of mussle culture in waste waters or sulfide-polluted areas for converting noxious sulfide into useful biomass, while algae-containing species yield more protein by absorbing (excessive eutrophic) nitrogen compounds from the water column. Both groups of symbiotic bivalves are assumed to be combined advantageously with other mariculture projects. Hence, this inventory is meant to serve in the development of Philippine aquaculture. (DBO)},
